        text: 'Winter arrives. The berry bush still smells sweet from fallen leaves, so your dopamine neurons fire a burst at the scent. You walk fifty meters through the cold, burning precious calories, and find zero berries.\n\nTo survive, the brain must calculate a negative error: "I expected 10 calories and got zero. Do not walk here again." Here lies the fatal flaw of a burst-only design: a neuron cannot fire fewer than zero spikes per second. At a 0 Hz baseline, the brain can celebrate surprises, but is completely blind to disappointment.\n\nThe evolutionary fix is to keep dopamine neurons humming at a steady 4 to 5 spikes per second all the time. This [[tonic dopamine]] baseline gives the cell room to drop. When a promised reward is missing, the lateral {{habenula}} briefly silences the {{vta}} down to 0 Hz, and that sudden pause erases the bad habit.',
