        text: 'A phasic dopamine burst lasts only 200 milliseconds. What if the berry bush sits at the top of a ten-minute hill climb? You cannot fire massive phasic bursts for ten minutes straight without flooding the synapse and blinding the animal to new surprises along the way.\n\nBecause physical movement burns calories, the {{striatum}} is built with a default brake pedal held down via the {{globus-pallidus}}. Without a continuous chemical signal to hold that brake off, movement freezes.\n\nThe evolutionary fix places two different receivers side by side on the receiving spine. High-affinity [[d2 receptor|D2 receptors]] are sensitive enough to catch the quiet 4.5 Hz tonic hum, holding the brake pedal off so a ten-minute climb feels manageable. Low-affinity [[d1 receptor|D1 receptors]] ignore the quiet hum and wake up only when a massive phasic burst hits them, punching the gas pedal.',
